INT. NIGHT TRAIN "TRAMONTANA," SLEEPER CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Brass fixtures, a narrow carpet, the rattle of rails. Lamplight swings. Every door is closed except one.

A STRANGER, thirties, in a gray travel coat, stands outside compartment 7 with his forehead against the glass, watching the dark countryside slide by. NOOR HADDAD, thirties, comes out of compartment 8 holding a phone to her ear and a paper cup in the other hand. She nearly collides with him.

                    NOOR
          Sorry!

                    STRANGER
          My fault.

She grins, waves him off, and lifts the phone again.

                    LUZIA (V.O.)
          (tinny, through phone)
          Noor? Are you eating?

                    NOOR
          Mãe, I am eating.

                    LUZIA (V.O.)
          What are you eating?

Noor looks at her cup. It holds three olives.

                    NOOR
          Lunch.

                    LUZIA (V.O.)
          It's midnight.

She covers the phone. The stranger is still there, listening, trying not to smile.

                    STRANGER
          Is that your mother?

                    NOOR
          Everyone's mother.

                    STRANGER
          Mine calls me twice a day. It's a record.

                    NOOR
          What's your name, or should I keep calling you "the man who watches trains"?

                    STRANGER
          Emilio.

                    NOOR
          Noor.

They shake hands, awkward with the cup, and a tiny olive rolls out onto the carpet. Both bend to pick it up and knock heads.

                    EMILIO
          Ow.

                    NOOR
          Ow!

                    LUZIA (V.O.)
          Noor! What was that noise?

                    NOOR
          Nothing, Mãe, a man.

                    LUZIA (V.O.)
          Is he handsome?

Noor puts the phone against her chest and Emilio pretends to read the evacuation sign. The train lurches. The door of compartment 7 slides open and BARNABY, sixty, in a conductor's cap, steps out with a clipboard.

                    BARNABY
          Tickets, please. Both of you. Wait. Both of you are in the wrong car.

Noor and Emilio look at each other, then at the tickets they are holding, then at the long corridor.

                    EMILIO
          So we're lost.

                    NOOR
          Together.

Barnaby clears his throat and stamps both tickets with a firm thump, and neither of them argues.
